        },
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.3758/s13428-020-01364-w",
          "targetDoi": "10.3758/s13428-013-0422-2",
          "snippet": "Second, OpenMATB should be able to interface with other free software toolboxes in order to communicate with physiological devices. For instance, Dalmaijer, Mathôt, and Van der Stigchel (2014) designed PyGaze, a toolbox allowing programmers to easily connect their software to a wide range of different makes of eye-trackers. Venthur and Blankertz (2012) developed Mushu, an acquisition library that makes it possible to receive neurophysiological data from any equipment (EEG, NIRS, or fMRI).",
          "section": "results",
          "type": "mentioning"
        },
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.3758/s13428-020-01364-w",
          "targetDoi": "10.3758/s13428-018-01193-y",
          "snippet": "By observing when the signal is received in the external equipment, it is possible to precisely synchronize the physiological parameters with the OpenMATB scenarios. This approach is the most common way to perform online synchronization with any external device, as in done, for example, in PsychoPy (Peirce et al, 2019). The second plug-in (smieyetracker) demonstrates how to communicate data to an external device, in this case an SMI eye-tracker.",
          "section": "openmatb architecture",
          "type": "mentioning"
        }
      ],
      "isOa": true,
      "fulltextExcerpts": [
        "These requirements therefore lie at the heart of the OpenMATB architecture, which is presented in the next section.\n\nOpenMATB architecture\n\nOpenMATB is a cross-platform software development programmed in Python (2.7). It is covered by a free software license to promote exchange between researchers, granting them the permission not only to run and study the source code, but also to share their software modifications.",
        "This latter capability is at the core of OpenMATB's response to the issue of replicability. To replicate an experiment, it is sufficient to request the corresponding script file and then use it with the relevant OpenMATB version.\n\nOverview of the script format\n\nTo illustrate the text file format, we replicated an extract of Parasuraman et al.'s (1993) study.",
        "s (1993) modified version of MATB and the replication performed using OpenMATB (Fig. 5) shows the versatility of the software and its ability to promote task customization, software extensibility, and experiment replicability.\n\nConclusions\n\nIn this article, we have presented the main features of OpenMATB."
